schw.1951.0.2.reviewed_statement:
Denote by $H$ a set of sequences $S = \{s_n\}_{n=1}^{\infty}$ of real numbers having the following properties:

(i) If $S = \{s_n\}_{n=1}^{\infty} \in H$, then $S' = \{s_n\}_{n=2}^{\infty} \in H$;

(ii) If $S = \{s_n\}_{n=1}^{\infty} \in H$ and $T = \{t_n\}_{n=1}^{\infty} \in H$, then $S + T = \{s_n + t_n\}_{n=1}^{\infty} \in H$ and $ST = \{s_nt_n\}_{n=1}^{\infty} \in H$;

(iii) $\{-1, -1, \ldots, -1, \ldots\} \in H$.

A real-valued function $f(S)$ defined on $H$ is called a quasi-limit of $S$ if it has the following properties: $$\begin{gathered}\text{If } S=\{c,c,\ldots,c,\ldots\},\ \text{then } f(S)=c;\\ \text{If } s_i\geqq 0,\ \text{then } f(S)\geqq 0;\\ f(S+T)=f(S)+f(T);\\ f(ST)=f(S)f(T);\\ f(S')=f(S).\end{gathered}$$ Prove that for every $S$, the quasi-limit $f(S)$ is an accumulation point of $S$.

schw.1957.0.5.reviewed_statement:
Find the continuous solutions of the functional equation $f(xyz) = f(x) + f(y) + f(z)$ in the following two cases:

(a) $x, y, z$ are arbitrary non-zero real numbers;

(b) $a < x, y, z < b$ ($1 < a^3 < b$).

schw.1958.0.10.reviewed_statement:
Prove that the function $$f(x)=\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}\left(\frac{\sin\vartheta}{\vartheta}\right)^{2k}\cos 2x\vartheta\,d\vartheta,$$ where $k$ is a positive integer, satisfies the following conditions:

(i) $f(x) = 0$ if $|x| \geqq k$ and $f(x) \geqq 0$ elsewhere;

(ii) in every interval $(l, l+1)$ ($l = -k, -k+1, \ldots, k-1$) the function $f(x)$ is a polynomial of degree $2k - 1$ at most.

schw.1997.0.1.reviewed_statement:
For every positive integer $k$ define a class $\mathcal{G}_k$ of graphs as follows. A graph $G = (V, E)$ belongs to $\mathcal{G}_k$ if and only if there is a colouring $\psi\colon E \to [k] = \{1, 2, \ldots, k\}$ of its edges such that for an arbitrary colouring $\phi\colon V \to [k]$ of the vertices the graph has an edge $e = \{x, y\}$ for which $\phi(x) = \phi(y) = \psi(e)$. Prove that there exist positive constants $c_1 < c_2$ with the following two properties:

(a) every graph in $\mathcal{G}_k$ has at least $c_1k^2$ vertices;

(b) there is a graph in $\mathcal{G}_k$ which has at most $c_2k^2$ vertices.

schw.2000.0.1.reviewed_statement:
Show that there is a function $f\colon [\omega_1]^2 \to \omega_1$ for which

(i) $f(\alpha,\beta) < \min\{\alpha,\beta\}$ if $\min\{\alpha,\beta\} > 0$;

(ii) if $\alpha_0 < \alpha_1 < \cdots < \alpha_i < \cdots < \omega_1$, then $\sup\{\alpha_i : i < \omega\} = \sup\{f(\alpha_i,\alpha_j) : i, j < \omega\}$.

schw.2002.0.6.reviewed_statement:
Let $K \subseteq \mathbb{R}$ be compact. Prove that the following two statements are equivalent:

(a) for every point $x$ of $K$ there is an uncountable set $F_x \subseteq \mathbb{R}$ such that $$\operatorname{dist}(F_x, F_y) \ge |x - y|$$ holds for every $x, y \in K$;

(b) $K$ has measure zero.

schw.2009.0.5.reviewed_statement:
Let $G$ be a finite non-commutative group of order $t = 2^nm$, where $n, m$ are positive integers and $m$ is odd. Prove that if the group contains an element of order $2^n$, then

(i) $G$ is not a simple group;

(ii) $G$ contains a normal subgroup of order $m$.

schw.2009.0.7.reviewed_statement:
Let $H$ be an arbitrary subgroup of the diffeomorphism group $\mathsf{Diff}^\infty(M)$ of a differentiable manifold $M$. We say that a $\mathcal{C}^\infty$ vector field $X$ is weakly tangent to the group $H$ if there exist a positive integer $k$ and a $\mathcal{C}^\infty$-differentiable map $\varphi\colon (-\varepsilon,\varepsilon)^k \times M \to M$ such that

(i) for fixed $t_1, \ldots, t_k$ the map $$\varphi_{t_1,\ldots,t_k}\colon x \in M \mapsto \varphi(t_1, \ldots, t_k, x)$$ is a diffeomorphism of $M$, and $\varphi_{t_1,\ldots,t_k} \in H$;

(ii) $\varphi_{t_1,\ldots,t_k} = \mathsf{Id}$ if $t_j = 0$ for some $1 \le j \le k$;

(iii) for an arbitrary $\mathcal{C}^\infty$ function $f\colon M \to \mathbb{R}$ $$Xf = \left.\frac{\partial^k(f\circ\varphi_{t_1,\ldots,t_k})}{\partial t_1\cdots\partial t_k}\right|_{(t_1,\ldots,t_k)=(0,\ldots,0)}.$$ Prove that the commutators of $\mathcal{C}^\infty$ vector fields weakly tangent to the group $H \subset \mathsf{Diff}^\infty(M)$ are also weakly tangent to $H$.
